WEBVTT

0:00:01.200 --> 0:00:03.900
<v Speaker 1>Demo Audio Studios.

0:00:04.100 --> 0:00:05.000
<v Speaker 2>Podcasts.

0:00:05.300 --> 0:00:06.100
<v Speaker 1>Radio.

0:00:06.400 --> 0:00:07.200
<v Speaker 2>News.

0:00:08.000 --> 0:00:12.400
<v Speaker 2>So picture this. You've got a boarding pass, you've got a window seat,

0:00:12.400 --> 0:00:17.100
<v Speaker 2>and the gate agent gets on the mic and says, we're looking for volunteers.

0:00:17.500 --> 0:00:21.800
<v Speaker 1>And everybody in the gate area suddenly finds something very interesting on their phone.

0:00:22.200 --> 0:00:26.900
<v Speaker 2>Right. Nobody makes eye contact. Because we all know what's coming next.

0:00:27.300 --> 0:00:31.600
<v Speaker 3>What's coming next is that the airline sold more seats than the plane actually has.

0:00:32.000 --> 0:00:36.200
<v Speaker 3>On purpose. Every single day. And from the inside, that's not a scandal.

0:00:36.400 --> 0:00:38.100
<v Speaker 3>It's a spreadsheet.

0:00:44.000 --> 0:00:48.300
<v Speaker 1>I'm Dana Whitfield, and this is Demo Take. Today on the show,

0:00:48.300 --> 0:00:53.000
<v Speaker 1>reporters Priya Nair and Tom Okafor walk us through the strange math of overbooking,

0:00:53.000 --> 0:00:57.400
<v Speaker 1>why airlines swear by it, and what happens when the bet goes sideways.

0:01:02.500 --> 0:01:06.100
<v Speaker 1>Priya, let's start with the obvious question. Why do they do this at all?

0:01:06.500 --> 0:01:11.300
<v Speaker 2>Because people don't show up. On a typical flight, somewhere between five and ten percent

0:01:11.300 --> 0:01:16.000
<v Speaker 2>of ticketed passengers just don't turn up. They miss a connection, they change plans,

0:01:16.000 --> 0:01:20.700
<v Speaker 2>they oversleep. And an empty seat on a plane that has already taken off is worth exactly zero.

0:01:21.100 --> 0:01:24.000
<v Speaker 1>So the airline is essentially betting against its own customers.

0:01:24.300 --> 0:01:29.600
<v Speaker 2>Kind of, yeah. They look at years of data for that specific route, that day of the week,

0:01:29.600 --> 0:01:34.100
<v Speaker 2>even that time of day, and they come up with a number. Sell this many extra tickets,

0:01:34.100 --> 0:01:37.800
<v Speaker 2>and nine times out of ten, everyone who shows up gets a seat.

0:01:38.200 --> 0:01:40.600
<v Speaker 1>And the tenth time?

0:01:41.000 --> 0:01:46.400
<v Speaker 3>The tenth time is where I come in. Because the tenth time is where it gets expensive and ugly.

0:01:46.800 --> 0:01:52.300
<v Speaker 3>When the bet goes wrong, the airline has to buy its way out. That's the auction at the gate.

0:01:52.700 --> 0:01:57.900
<v Speaker 3>They start low, a couple hundred dollars in vouchers, and they keep bumping it up until somebody bites.

0:01:58.300 --> 0:02:01.700
<v Speaker 1>I've seen those numbers get pretty high. Like, four figures high.

0:02:02.100 --> 0:02:07.000
<v Speaker 3>They can. And here's the thing, that's still cheaper for the airline than flying with empty seats

0:02:07.000 --> 0:02:11.800
<v Speaker 3>on every other flight that week. The occasional payout is just the cost of doing business.

0:02:12.200 --> 0:02:16.300
<v Speaker 1>Okay, but it's not just money, right? There's a reputational hit every time this makes the news.

0:02:16.700 --> 0:02:21.900
<v Speaker 3>Huge. Every airline has a story they'd rather forget about a passenger being dragged off a plane,

0:02:21.900 --> 0:02:26.400
<v Speaker 3>and after one of those, the whole industry pulled back for a while.

0:02:26.800 --> 0:02:31.200
<v Speaker 2>And that's the interesting part. They pulled back, but they didn't stop. Not even close.

0:02:31.600 --> 0:02:36.800
<v Speaker 2>What they did instead was get smarter about it. Better forecasting, bigger incentives earlier,

0:02:36.800 --> 0:02:41.300
<v Speaker 2>so that by the time you get to the gate, the problem has mostly been solved quietly.

0:02:41.700 --> 0:02:45.400
<v Speaker 1>So the practice didn't go away, it just went underground.

0:02:45.800 --> 0:02:50.600
<v Speaker 2>Pretty much. If you've ever gotten an email the night before a flight offering you a few hundred dollars

0:02:50.600 --> 0:02:54.200
<v Speaker 2>to switch to a later departure, that's overbooking. It just has better manners now.

0:02:54.600 --> 0:02:58.900
<v Speaker 1>Tom, bottom line. Is this ever going to go away?

0:02:59.300 --> 0:03:04.700
<v Speaker 3>Honestly, no. As long as people miss flights, there's money on the table, and airlines are not in the habit

0:03:04.700 --> 0:03:09.000
<v Speaker 3>of leaving money on the table. The only thing that changes is how gracefully they handle the fallout.

0:03:09.400 --> 0:03:13.600
<v Speaker 1>Which, to be fair, is a pretty low bar.

0:03:14.000 --> 0:03:16.200
<v Speaker 3>It's a very low bar.

0:03:20.500 --> 0:03:25.300
<v Speaker 1>This is Demo Take from Demo News. It was hosted by me, Dana Whitfield, and reported by Priya Nair

0:03:25.300 --> 0:03:30.100
<v Speaker 1>and Tom Okafor. Our producer is Sam Reyes. If you liked the episode, leave us a review,

0:03:30.100 --> 0:03:32.400
<v Speaker 1>and we'll see you tomorrow.
